{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1793011507,"nonce":"AEfQJKvlfF/xqIjjJ17xSScVDD0=","message":"Live browser-session measurement on October 26, PID 78589 (indexing release 61b91acf): vmmap physical footprint 428.7 MiB, peak 552.2 MiB, 355.8 MiB allocated in 4,360,425 malloc objects, and 69.1 MiB fragmentation. Most writable memory was compressed/swapped at the sample. Stack reservations total 524.1 MiB but only 256 KiB was committed, so reserved stack size does not explain the large footprint. Editor-response retention is bounded to 32 entries / 64 MiB of estimated heap; completed compiler sessions are still bounded by graph count (default two), not by bytes. Graphs retain AST/TAST, symbols/types, interned strings, per-file indexes, source copies and macro state; workspace declarations/docs and dirty overlays remain separate live owners. vmmap does not attribute these bytes to Rust types, so their precise shares remain unmeasured. Next retention work should combine an approximate byte budget and idle expiry for completed graphs with file-backed editor responses, then measure real-project cold dependency reuse before dropping the last reusable graph indiscriminately.","files":null}]}